+# Block placement: performance review
+
+Reviewed: `src/ServerScriptService/BlockPlacement.server.luau` and
+`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/BlockPlacer.luau`, against a list of six suggested
+optimizations.
+
+## Starting point
+
+- **Scale.** The server accepts at most one placement per player every 0.1 s, and a server holds
+  8 players. That caps the server at 80 placements a second, and real play will be far below
+  that.
+- **Symptoms.** None. Nobody has reported lag, and no profile points at placement.
+- **Cost per placement.** The server runs a few type checks, one table lookup, one distance
+  check, and creates one anchored Part. The client does one raycast per click or tap. At the
+  rate cap, creating parts takes a small fraction of a millisecond per second of server time.
+
+Rule applied: with no measured problem and a cost this small, the plain version stays. A change
+is worth making only if it fixes a correctness or design problem, or makes the code simpler.
+
+## Verdicts
+
+| # | Suggestion | Verdict |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Pool block Parts | No |
+| 2 | Pack PlaceBlock arguments into a buffer | No |
+| 3 | Switch PlaceBlock to UnreliableRemoteEvent | No, and it would cause bugs |
+| 4 | Add `--!native` to both scripts | No |
+| 5 | Replace `table.find` with a lookup table | Done, as a simplification |
+| 6 | Create RaycastParams once | No |
+
+### 1. Pool block Parts: no
+
+A pool saves time when instances are created *and destroyed* often, so a returned instance can be
+reused. Placed blocks are never removed, so nothing would ever return to the pool. The pool
+would just be a stock of pre-made parts that has to be refilled with `Instance.new` anyway.
+
+Even for objects that do churn, reusing a plain Part measured at about a third of the cost of
+destroying and re-creating it. That gain matters at hundreds of creations a second, not at 80 or
+fewer. Meanwhile a server-side pool adds idle instances that replicate to every client at join,
+needs reset code for every property a block type sets (`Color`, `Material`, `Transparency`, the
+`PlacedBy` attribute), and parked parts stay in physics and render bookkeeping.
+
+Revisit if blocks become breakable *and* a profile shows instance creation or destruction in the
+frame.
+
+### 2. Buffer for PlaceBlock arguments: no
+
+Each call sends a short block name and a `Vector3`: about 9 bytes of remote overhead plus
+roughly 20 bytes of arguments. At the cap of 10 a second per player, that is well under 1 KB/s
+per client, which is negligible. Buffers save bytes and memory, not CPU time, and here there are
+almost no bytes to save.
+
+A buffer would also make the server handler harder to secure: it would have to check the buffer
+length and decode by hand before the existing type and NaN checks could run, which adds places
+for an exploit to slip through.
+
+### 3. UnreliableRemoteEvent: no, and it would break placement
+
+Unreliable remotes are not "faster" in any way a player would notice for a single click. What
+they do is drop packets and deliver out of order. They are meant for continuous data where the
+next update replaces the last one, such as positions or aim.
+
+A placement is a one-off action that must arrive. With an unreliable remote, a click on a lossy
+connection would sometimes place nothing, with no error and no retry. Excess unreliable calls
+are also dropped instead of delayed. This would change behavior for players and is the one item
+on the list that would introduce bugs.
+
+### 4. `--!native`: no
+
+Native code generation speeds up number-crunching Luau: tight math loops, buffer work. These
+scripts run a handful of checks and then call engine APIs (`Instance.new`, property writes,
+`Workspace:Raycast`, `FireServer`), and native compilation does nothing for time spent inside
+the engine. They also run at most a few times a second, not every frame.
+
+Native code costs compile time and memory against a limit shared by the whole game. On the client
+it is only supported on some platforms, so `BlockPlacer.luau` would see no effect on most
+devices.
+
+### 5. Replace `table.find` with a lookup table: done, but for simplicity, not speed
+
+The speed argument does not hold up. `table.find` over five names takes nanoseconds, and a set
+only wins for large lists in hot loops.
+
+The change was still worth making, because the lookup table already existed. The handler
+checked the name with `table.find(VALID_NAMES, ...)` and then looked up the same name in
+`BlockTypes.ByName` on the next line. `VALID_NAMES` was a second copy of the block type list.
+The handler now looks the name up in `BlockTypes.ByName` and returns when the result is `nil`, and
+`VALID_NAMES` is gone. Behavior is unchanged: the same names are accepted, and the same names are
+rejected.
+
+The client's `table.find(NUMBER_KEYS, input.KeyCode)` stays as it is. It runs on key presses
+over a five-item list, and it reads clearly.
+
+### 6. Create RaycastParams once: no
+
+`RaycastParams.new()` runs once per click or tap. That is one small allocation per user action,
+which is far too rare to show up in a profile or in garbage collection.
+
+The current version is also correct in a way a cached one has to work for. The filter excludes
+the *current* character, and the character is replaced on every respawn. A params object created
+once would need to be updated on `CharacterAdded`, or it would keep filtering a destroyed
+character and let raycasts hit the new one. That extra bookkeeping buys nothing measurable.
+
+## What would change these verdicts
+
+Measure first if any of these happen:
+
+- Players report lag or stutter while building. Take a MicroProfiler capture on the server while
+  several players place blocks, and look for instance creation or replication labels.
+- Blocks become breakable, or the rate limit goes up a lot. Then instance churn becomes real,
+  and pooling (on the client, for visuals) may be worth measuring.
+- Servers get much bigger, or block counts grow into the tens of thousands. At that point the
+  macro question is the total instance count and join time (consider streaming, or storing
+  blocks as data and building visuals on clients), not any of the six items above.
